Notes:
	Notes in double brackets map scenes to game beats, room states and mechanics. Most Fountain apps hide them when printing.

# TEASER

FADE IN:

.THE LATE ROOM — ON A CRT TELEVISION

[[Frame. Plays on Room 14's CRT. Candidate title/attract screen for the game.]]

Grain. Scanlines. A black-and-white set dressed as a motel lobby: a front desk, a brass bell, a pegboard of room keys on hooks. One hook is empty.

THE NIGHT MANAGER (60s, gray suit, a white carnation, the patience of furniture) stands behind the desk as if he has always stood there.

NIGHT MANAGER
Good evening. There is a kind of room that only exists after midnight. You've stayed in one. The bedspread knows something about you. The ice is somehow warm. And the mirror is never quite as glad to see you as you are to see it.

He taps the bell. It rings a little too long.

NIGHT MANAGER
This is the Dellwood. Thirty rooms, one vacancy, and a policy, posted in the office, that a story is not over until both parties agree. Tonight's guest has come to settle an argument. He believes there are two sides to every story.
(beat)
The Dellwood keeps four.

He turns a heavy guest ledger toward us. A name in a careful hand: THOREAU.

NIGHT MANAGER
Room 14. Checkout is when you agree.

The picture collapses to a white dot.

EXT. ROUTE 9 — BUS SHELTER — NIGHT

Cicadas. A sodium streetlight hums over a fiberglass bus shelter on a two-lane road that hasn't seen a car in a long time. The heat of an August that has gone on too long.

A sign: ROUTE 9 — DELLWOOD. Below it, in marker: NOT IN SERVICE.

THE COMMUTER (60s, pressed slacks, a briefcase on his knees) reads a paperback. He turns a page. He turns it back.

Headlights. An old city bus glides up, its interior lights stuttering, empty but for one passenger. The doors sigh open.

THOREAU (early 30s, Black, round gold glasses, mustache and goatee, charcoal jacket over a gray tee, a thin chain) steps down. A faded bruise at his left temple. He holds a small card.

The doors close. The bus pulls away and is simply not there anymore. No taillights. Just road.

The Commuter lowers his book.

COMMUTER
That bus doesn't run.

THOREAU
I was just on it.

COMMUTER
I've been waiting for it eleven years.
(beat)
How was it?

THOREAU
(considers)
Clean.

COMMUTER
(nods, devastated, grateful)
I always figured it'd be clean.

He goes back to the same page.

INSERT — THE CARD

A Dellwood reservation card. Handwriting with loops and a heavy hand on the downstroke:

>ROOM 14. COME TELL IT RIGHT. — D.<

THOREAU (V.O.)
Her handwriting leans like it's about to leave the page. It always did. I'd have known it in the dark. Which is good, because that's where I was.

EXT. ROUTE 9 — CROSSWALK — CONTINUOUS

Two empty lanes, painted stripes, a blinking yellow light blinking for nobody.

On the corner stands OTIS PRUITT (11): orange crossing-guard sash, a hand-held STOP sign taller than he is, a clicker counter on a lanyard. He stands at attention as though rush hour is about to break over him.

Thoreau steps to the curb. Otis raises a flat palm. Thoreau stops.

Nothing is coming. Nothing is coming for miles.

OTIS
Not yet.

A long beat. Cicadas. Otis consults a wristwatch with no hands.

OTIS
Okay.

He marches into the road, sign high, and ushers Thoreau across.

OTIS
Walk, don't run. Pass four hundred and thirteen.

*Click.*

THOREAU
Four hundred and thirteen people? Tonight?

OTIS
Since August.

THOREAU
It's August.

OTIS
(patiently)
Since *the* August.

They reach the far curb. Otis looks up at him and studies his hands.

OTIS
You're the one with no camera.

THOREAU
I don't own a camera.

OTIS
(writes on the back of his own hand with a pen)
That's what the other one said.
(beat)
He was holding one, though.

Otis returns to his corner and resumes attention.

Across the street, above a closed laundromat, a curtain is drawn shut. A second later it is drawn shut again.

EXT. THE DELLWOOD — NIGHT

A two-story motor lodge, 1960s modern gone soft at the edges. A neon sign: THE DELLWOOD. Beneath it, VACANCY, with a NO in front of it that flickers on and off and can't decide.

A drained swimming pool in the courtyard. A thin seam of teal light from somewhere teal light should not come from.

INT. DELLWOOD — FRONT OFFICE — NIGHT

[[LOC_DELLWOOD_OFFICE_001 · the arrival ritual · the key exchange]]

Wood paneling. A brass desk bell. A pegboard of keys on black diamond tags, the numbers worn soft. Framed on the wall behind the desk: a typewritten placard.

NETTIE (70s, cardigan, reading glasses on a chain, the composure of someone who has outlasted every previous owner and possibly the building) sits behind the desk doing a crossword in pen.

A bell over the door jingles as Thoreau enters. She doesn't look up.

NETTIE
You'll want to ring.

THOREAU
You can see me.

NETTIE
I can. The bell can't.

He rings the desk bell. Satisfied, she sets down her pen and looks at him for the first time. Something flickers in her face: recognition, or a professional absence of it.

NETTIE
Welcome to the Dellwood. Name?

THOREAU
Thoreau. There should be a reservation.

He slides the card across. She reads it longer than a card needs to be read. Then she opens the GUEST LEDGER, a heavy book with columns ruled in red. Many lines are blacked out with thick redaction bars, neat as a government's.

NETTIE
Thoreau. Fourteen.

She turns the ledger toward him and offers the pen.

NETTIE
Arrival signature.

On the line where he's meant to sign, someone already has: THOREAU, in his name and almost his hand. Faster and more confident. The ink is wet. It smears under his thumb.

THOREAU
Someone's already signed.

NETTIE
Then you're expected. Sign under it.

He hesitates, then signs below it. His signature looks careful next to the other one, like a copy of a copy. He can't tell which one is the copy.

Nettie rises and goes to the pegboard. Hook 14 holds a brass key, and behind it on the same hook hangs a second key 14, identical.

She gives him the front one and leaves the other.

THOREAU
There are two.

NETTIE
We only have the one key fourteen. That's why there are two.

THOREAU
That doesn't —

NETTIE
(points the pen at the placard, kindly)
House rules.

INSERT — THE PLACARD

>THE DELLWOOD — HOUSE RULES<
>1. ROOM 14 IS A TWO-PERSON ROOM.<
>2. MIRRORS COUNT.<
>3. PLEASE KEEP THE LAMP ON.<
>4. CHECKOUT IS WHEN YOU AGREE.<

BACK TO SCENE

THOREAU
What time is checkout?

NETTIE
When you agree.

THOREAU
Agree with who?

Nettie sits and picks up the crossword.

NETTIE
Eleven across. "What love edits out." Five letters.

THOREAU
I don't know.

NETTIE
(writing nothing)
Nobody does, the first night.

Thoreau opens his hand. The key: the number 14 worn soft but still legible, a black diamond tag.

THOREAU (V.O.)
A key is a promise somebody made to a door. I was about to find out who.

SMASH CUT TO:

>**L I K E N E S S**<

>"A NIGHT FOR FOUR"<

===

# ACT ONE

>ACT ONE<

INT. DELLWOOD HALLWAY — NIGHT

[[LOC_DELLWOOD_HALLWAY_001 · threshold, repetition, impossible doors]]

A long carpeted hallway. Tungsten sconces every ten feet; every third one is dead. Paneled doors: 9, 10, 11, 12, 13. The carpet repeats a diamond pattern, the same shape as the key tag, and it repeats slightly too often.

THOREAU (V.O.)
Every motel hallway is exactly as long as the reason you came. This one was long.

He passes 13. The next door is 14. He keeps walking, because there is another door farther down.

It is also 14.

He stops, looks back at the first 14, then ahead at the second. The HVAC exhales somewhere in the walls.

He looks down at his key, then between the two doors. Under the nearer door is a thin bar of warm lamplight, and behind it a woman's voice, under her breath:

DRYA (O.S.)
— five, six, seven, eight.

Under the farther door there is only dark. It is the kind of dark that listens.

He knocks on the near one.

DRYA (O.S.)
It's open. It's been open.

He uses the key anyway. It turns.

INT. ROOM 14 — NIGHT

[[BEAT_ARRIVAL_001 · STATE_00_NORMAL · MECH_INSPECT_001 begins]]

The chamber.

A queen bed with a quilted headboard. A nightstand with a tungsten lamp, a clock-radio with a cassette deck, and a red rotary phone. A CRT television on the dresser. A slatted closet. A small desk and chair by a curtained window. The bathroom door ajar: a fluorescent ring light, a mirror over the sink. Faded botanical wallpaper. Everything is a little too clean for the building it's in.

On the nightstand, beside the lamp, sits THE DEVICE: palm-sized, smooth and black, without a seam or a button, like a river stone that has learned to sit very still.

DRYA (late 20s, Black, warm medium-brown skin, long dark curls with braids along the front hairline, small gold hoops, a nose ring, fitted dark top, dark trousers, barefoot) sits at the end of the bed. Her hands mark a phrase of choreography in the air, small and exact.

DRYA
— five, six, seven, eight.

She stops and looks at him. It's a long look, and neither of them knows what to do with it.

DRYA
You came.

THOREAU
You asked.

DRYA
(a breath that could be a laugh)
I didn't.

She holds up a Dellwood reservation card. Small, square, careful capitals:

>ROOM 14. COME TELL IT RIGHT. — T.<

He holds up his. They compare across the room without getting any closer.

THOREAU
That's my handwriting.

DRYA
And that's mine.

THOREAU
I didn't write this.

DRYA
Then either we're both very good at forgery, or somebody wanted us in the same room.

THOREAU
Somebody.

DRYA
(a glance at the device on the nightstand)
Something.

THOREAU
How long have you been here?

DRYA
Since three-fourteen.

He looks at the clock-radio. 3:14. It doesn't change.

THOREAU (V.O.)
The last time I saw her it was three-fourteen too, by the clock over our stove. The night we ended. A year ago, maybe more. Grief keeps bad time.

[[BEAT_ARGUMENT_RESIDUE_001 · STATE_01_RESIDUE · inspect: coffee, locket, phone]]

She stands. Even crossing a motel room, she moves with a dancer's economy; there isn't a wasted step in her.

At the dresser there's a motel coffee maker and two paper-wrapped cups.

DRYA
There's coffee. It's terrible. I made it anyway.

She pours two cups, tears open three sugar packets into one, and stirs.

THOREAU
Since when?

DRYA
Since when what?

THOREAU
Sugar. You take it black.

DRYA
I've always taken it sweet.

She holds his eyes and sips. Something crosses her face, a tiny wince, there and gone, like a sound from another room.

THOREAU (V.O.)
She has never taken it sweet. I did. Early on I used to make hers like mine, and she drank it anyway, to be kind, and I didn't find out for a year. That was the first thing the room got wrong. Or the first thing it remembered right.

She hands him the black one. He takes it. Their fingers deliberately don't touch.

On the nightstand, next to the device, lies a small brass locket. He picks it up and opens it.

INSERT — THE TINTYPE

An old photograph on blackened metal. A man and a woman in Sunday clothes, 1890s, stiff, posed. They look like THOREAU and DRYA. Not like them. Them.

The woman is smiling. The man is not.

BACK TO SCENE

THOREAU
Is this —

DRYA
It was here when I got here. They look like us.

THOREAU
They look like us at a funeral.

DRYA
Look again.

INSERT — THE TINTYPE

Now the man is smiling and the woman is not. Neither image has moved. The smile has changed hands.

BACK TO SCENE

He closes the locket carefully, as if it might wake.

THOREAU
They can't agree on who was happy.

DRYA
Sounds familiar.

The red phone RINGS. It's a real bell, old and loud. They both stare at it. On the third ring Thoreau answers.

NETTIE (V.O.)
(through the receiver)
Front desk. Courtesy call. Room fourteen is reminded that checkout is when you agree.

THOREAU
Agree on what?

NETTIE (V.O.)
The night, Mr. Thoreau.
(beat)
We find it goes faster if you start at the beginning.

Click. Dial tone. And underneath the dial tone, very faintly, the hiss of a cassette.

He sets down the receiver.

THOREAU
The night.

DRYA
The apartment.

THOREAU
We don't have to —

DRYA
We have to.

She takes the desk chair, turns it to face the bed, and sits as formally as if she were giving a deposition.

DRYA
That's why we're here. Somebody wants it told right.

He sits on the edge of the bed, facing her. A room for two. It feels like a courtroom built for exactly two people.

DRYA
You first.

THOREAU
(a long beat)
I remember the clock over the stove. Three-fourteen. I remember your bag by the door, the green one, packed so neat it looked like it had been packed for a while. I remember saying your name.
(beat)
And then I remember the carpet.

DRYA
That's it?

THOREAU
There's a part in the middle where there should be a conversation. It's like a tape somebody recorded over. You can hear that something was there.

DRYA
Something was.

THOREAU
Your turn.

She sets the sweet coffee down and folds her hands. When she speaks it is fluent. Too fluent, like a dancer marking a combination she has done a thousand times.

DRYA
I came home angry. I'd been angry for weeks and I didn't tell you why. You asked me to sit down and talk, and I wouldn't. I said things to hurt you. I picked up your camera and I threw it, and it hit you here —
(touches her own left temple)
— and you went down, and I left. You were patient. You were always patient. I was cruel.
(beat)
That's what happened.

The lamp brightens. Warm, pleased, a shade too warm.

At the edge of the window, under the curtain, a vertical seam of TEAL light bleeds in, like a door opening somewhere it shouldn't.

Thoreau watches her. She's looking at her own hands as if they belong to someone else's story.

THOREAU
You don't believe that.

DRYA
I just said it.

THOREAU
You said it like a line.

DRYA
(quiet, unsettled by herself)
Everything I say about that night comes out like a line.

THOREAU
Drya. I don't own a camera.

DRYA
(as if it's the strangest sentence she's ever heard)
You never put it down.

He stands. He needs water, a door, anything.

INT. ROOM 14 — BATHROOM — CONTINUOUS

Fluorescent ring light, buzzing. White tile. A mirror over the sink.

He runs the tap, splashes his face, straightens and looks.

His reflection looks back at him. It's normal.

He takes off his glasses, rubs his eyes and puts them back on.

In the mirror, his reflection is still putting its glasses back on. One beat late.

He goes still. The reflection finishes the motion, then goes still too, late.

He leans closer. The reflection leans closer, late.

His eyes are open. In the mirror the reflected eyes are closed and its lips are moving, mid-word. At first there's no sound. Then, as if from far down a hallway, in his own voice:

REFLECTION (V.O.)
(faint)
...rewind it.

SMASH CUT TO BLACK.

>END OF ACT ONE<

===

# ACT TWO

>ACT TWO<

INT. ROOM 14 — BATHROOM — CONTINUOUS

[[BEAT_MIRROR_LAG_001 · STATE_02_MIRROR_LAG · MECH_MIRROR_LAG_001 · PROP_MIRROR_001]]

The fluorescent buzz comes back. He hasn't moved. The reflection is a reflection again. Almost.

He tests it. He raises his right hand; the reflection raises its hand, a beat late. He lowers it; the reflection lowers, late.

He holds perfectly still. The reflection keeps moving a moment longer: a small adjustment, a tired roll of the shoulders, the kind of thing a man does when nobody's directing him. Then it settles.

Drya stands in the doorway with her cup, watching. She isn't afraid.

DRYA
It's been doing that since I got here.

THOREAU
To you?

DRYA
Only to you. Mine's fine.

She steps in beside him. In the mirror her reflection is perfectly in time. His is a beat behind.

They stand side by side in the glass: a couple in a motel bathroom mirror, the most ordinary picture in the world, except that one of them is late.

THOREAU
Maybe I'm early.

DRYA
(soft)
You were always early. You'd get to things before they started and stand there like you were guarding them.

THOREAU (V.O.)
A man looks in a mirror to make sure he's still there. I checked, and I was there. I just wasn't first.

In the mirror, for a single frame, his reflection holds something at chest height: a small camcorder with its red light on.

In the room, his hands are empty.

He spins around. Nothing is behind him. He turns back. The reflection's hands are empty again, and late.

DRYA
What?

THOREAU
Nothing.
(beat)
We need ice.

DRYA
For what?

THOREAU
(touching his temple)
For this.

DRYA
That's a year old.

THOREAU
It still hurts.

She looks at the bruise, the way you'd look at an old photograph of a place you used to live.

DRYA
Yeah. It does.

INT. DELLWOOD HALLWAY — NIGHT

He steps out holding a plastic ice bucket.

The near 14 is behind him. At the far end of the hall the other 14 sits dark, and something on the other side of that door is very still in a way that's listening.

His jacket pocket is warm. He reaches in. THE DEVICE is there; he doesn't remember taking it. One side is warm against his palm, the other cold, warm exactly where it shouldn't be.

He puts it back and walks the other way.

EXT. DELLWOOD — VENDING ALCOVE — NIGHT

A concrete alcove off the parking lot, lit blue-white by machines.

On the ice machine, a hand-lettered sign: OUT OF ORDER — MAKES MEMORIES ONLY.

Beside it stands OTIS, still in the sash, with a red wagon carrying a cooler of hand-cut ice blocks and a cash box.

OTIS
Ice is a quarter.

THOREAU
You're the crossing guard.

OTIS
I'm also the ice. It's a small town.
(re: the machine)
That one only makes memories now. Those are free, but you have to carry them yourself, and they don't keep anything cold.

Thoreau hands him a quarter. With tongs, Otis sets one clear block in the bucket.

Frozen inside the block, like a fly in amber, is a Polaroid.

Thoreau lifts the block to the light. Through the ice: DRYA laughing in a kitchen, holding a wooden spoon like a microphone, lit by warm lamplight. She's pure joy. Whoever took the picture isn't in it, but their shadow falls across the floor: a man's shadow with a camera raised to his face.

THOREAU
Where did you get this?

OTIS
It was in the ice.

THOREAU
How long has it been in the ice?

OTIS
Since August.

THOREAU
Which —

OTIS
*The* August.
(patiently, as though for the hundredth time)
Mister, I keep telling everybody. There's only been the one.

At the next machine stands DESMOND (late 30s, Black, a fresh line-up, a motel bathrobe over basketball shorts, slides), feeding a dollar into a vending machine that doesn't sell candy. Behind its glass, in each coil, hangs a brass key with a paper tag: SPARE. NOT YOURS. THE GOOD YEAR. B-SIDE.

DESMOND
(not looking over)
Only machine in this whole town that works, and it only sells keys.

THOREAU
Keys to what?

DESMOND
That's the thing. It don't say.

The machine spits his dollar back out. He smooths it on his robe and tries again.

DESMOND
You in fourteen?

THOREAU
How'd you know?

DESMOND
You got the walk. Everybody in fourteen walks like they're about to be asked to explain themselves.

The coil turns and a key drops: B-SIDE. He pockets it like a man who collects them.

DESMOND
I'm in nine. Been in nine for a minute.

THOREAU
How long's a minute?

DESMOND
Depends who's counting. Kid out there says four hundred. I say my deposit.
(leans on the machine)
You know what this place is, right?

THOREAU
A motel.

DESMOND
(laughs, genuinely)
Okay. Yeah. A motel.
(beat)
Dellwood's where people come to try and get their deposit back on a relationship. You bring what's left. Pictures. A song. The way she said your name when she was annoyed. You tell it to the little rock on the nightstand, and it keeps it for you. Keeps it real good.

THOREAU
And then what?

DESMOND
Some folks say it gives it back.
(beat)
I wouldn't know. I'm still waiting on mine.

He looks at Thoreau properly for the first time, and something in his face sharpens.

DESMOND
Hold up. The last dude in fourteen had a camera. Filmed everything. Filmed the ice. Filmed this machine. Asked it if it loved him.

THOREAU
That wasn't me.

DESMOND
Nah. I know.
(squints)
He blinked late.

He pads off in his slides, talking over his shoulder.

DESMOND
And nobody gets the whole deposit back, by the way. That's the hustle. They keep a little.

EXT. DELLWOOD — COURTYARD POOL — NIGHT

Thoreau cuts back through the courtyard with the bucket on his hip, and stops.

In the drained swimming pool, standing in the deep end on cracked blue tile, is a WHITE HORSE.

It's calm. It's breathing. Steam lifts off its back in the heat. It looks up at him with total, uncomplicated attention.

Drya is already at the rail. She doesn't look at him; she's looking at the horse.

DRYA
It's been there since I got here. I keep waiting for it to want something.

THOREAU
Does it?

DRYA
No.
(beat)
That's the scariest thing in this whole town.

He comes and stands at the rail. They don't touch. The horse lowers its head and noses a dry leaf across the tile.

DRYA
We'd have liked it here. If we were the kind of people who went places for fun.

THOREAU
We went places.

DRYA
We went places so you could film them.

THOREAU
(gently, as if she's misremembering)
We went to the lake in June. You swam out to the buoy and back, and I timed you.

DRYA
(very still)
...You timed me.

THOREAU
Four minutes and change. You were so mad you didn't break four.

DRYA
I never told him I was —
(catches it)
I never told you I was mad about that.

THOREAU
You didn't have to.

She turns and really looks at him. It's the softest she has been all night, and it frightens them both.

DRYA
Where was your camera, Thoreau? At the lake.

THOREAU
I didn't —

DRYA
Where was it?

He thinks. He can see the lake, the buoy, her arms cutting the water. He can see his own hand holding a cheap plastic stopwatch.

THOREAU
I had a stopwatch.

DRYA
(barely)
He had a camera.

The horse breathes. The cicadas stop all at once, as if someone found the switch.

THOREAU
Who's "he"?

Across the road, above the laundromat, the curtain is drawn shut. A second later it is drawn shut again.

At the end of the block, under the last working streetlight, a MAN stands with his back to them: Thoreau's height, Thoreau's jacket.

He turns around slowly, too slowly, a beat behind some cue only he heard.

It's THOREAU. THE OTHER. The same face, glasses and goatee, but he stands like a man who hasn't slept in a year, and he holds a small camcorder at his chest with its red light glowing.

He raises the camcorder and points it at them.

Thoreau takes a step toward him. The Other stops, a beat after Thoreau stops. Thoreau steps back. The Other steps back, late.

The device in Thoreau's pocket is warm enough to feel through the fabric. It starts to hum, a low tone at the very bottom of hearing, like a radio between stations.

DRYA
(not taking her eyes off him)
Inside. Now. Keep the lamp on.

THOREAU
Why —

DRYA
House rules.

The Other lowers the camera and starts walking. He isn't walking toward them. He's walking toward the Dellwood's front door, like a man coming home.

>END OF ACT TWO<

===

# ACT THREE

>ACT THREE<

INT. ROOM 14 — NIGHT

They come in fast. Thoreau locks the door and slides the chain. Drya checks the lamp: on, warm.

The room has changed while they were gone, subtly. The bed is made, tight as a drum. The two coffee cups have been washed and set upside down on a folded towel. The locket is closed. The clock-radio's cassette door hangs open and empty.

On the pillow, squared to the edge, sits a stack of paper bound with a brass fastener.

INSERT — THE TITLE PAGE

Typed, in a typewriter face:

>A NIGHT FOR FOUR<
>written by<
>THOREAU<

BACK TO SCENE

THOREAU
I didn't write this.

DRYA
You keep saying that like it's going to start being true.

He turns the page.

INSERT — PAGE ONE

It's in screenplay format, and it's the scene we're in:

*INT. ROOM 14 — NIGHT. They come in fast. THOREAU locks the door. The room has changed while they were gone...*

BACK TO SCENE

He flips forward. A line near the bottom of a page:

*THOREAU reads the page. He does not believe it.*

He looks up. Drya is reading over his shoulder.

DRYA
Read the next one.

*DRYA finds the tape in the nightstand drawer. She plays it.*

She looks at the nightstand and opens the drawer. Where the Gideon Bible should be lies a cassette: worn consumer plastic, with a white label in small, square, careful capitals.

INSERT — THE LABEL

>TAPE 03 — FINAL CUT<

BACK TO SCENE

DRYA
Your handwriting.

THOREAU
It's my handwriting. It's not my —

DRYA
Say it. "I didn't write this."

He doesn't say it.

She slides the tape into the clock-radio and rests her thumb on PLAY. She looks at him, and he nods. She presses it.

[[BEAT_TAPE_CONTRADICTION_001 · STATE_04_TAPE_CONTRADICTION · PROP_TAPE_001 · the room edits itself]]

TAPE AUDIO: an apartment kitchen. A clock ticking. A refrigerator hum. Then his voice, warm, reasonable and gentle:

THOREAU (ON TAPE)
Drya. Can you sit down? I just want to talk.

DRYA (ON TAPE)
(cold, clipped)
I'm done talking to you.

THOREAU (ON TAPE)
I'm not angry. I just want to understand.

DRYA (ON TAPE)
You want to *understand.*

A CRASH. Something thrown.

As the tape plays, the room EDITS ITSELF behind them:

The botanical wallpaper behind the headboard peels away in one long curl, revealing older paper underneath: a mustard kitchen print. The apartment's.

The dresser is now a kitchen counter.

The CRT sits where the apartment's TV sat.

Under the desk, the carpet is the apartment's beige carpet, with a dark stain where a camcorder once lay.

The lamp burns warm, and warmer, a false sunset.

Thoreau turns slowly in place, watching his life be installed around him.

On the tape: a heavy THUD, a body hitting a floor. Silence. Then his voice, very close to the microphone, calm:

THOREAU (ON TAPE)
...Cut.

Click. Side A ends.

The room holds, half motel and half apartment, its seams showing like a set.

DRYA
(shaking)
That's not what I said.

THOREAU
It's your voice.

DRYA
It's my voice saying his words.

She ejects the tape and turns it over.

DRYA
There's always a B-side. He never threw anything away. He just moved it to the other side.

She slams it back in and presses PLAY.

TAPE AUDIO, SIDE B: the same kitchen and the same clock, rougher this time. Room tone. The whir of a camcorder motor running. No edit.

DRYA (ON TAPE)
(calm, exhausted, steady)
Put it down.

THOREAU (ON TAPE)
I'm just recording.

DRYA (ON TAPE)
You're always just recording. Put it down and look at me with your eyes.

THOREAU (ON TAPE)
(a laugh, colder than any voice we've heard from him)
You're better on tape. You know that? On tape I can fix your timing.

In the room, Thoreau flinches as if he's been struck. He has never heard his own voice like that.

DRYA (ON TAPE)
(very quiet)
You love the version of me you can rewind.

A long hiss. The motor whirs.

DRYA (ON TAPE)
I'm leaving. Don't film me leaving.

Footsteps. A door. Then a scramble: the camcorder jostling, running steps, a sharp crack of plastic and a hard THUD, a man groaning on the floor. Then a long, long stretch of nothing but the motor and the kitchen clock.

Then, from the floor, close to the mic, his voice, wet and hurt:

THOREAU (ON TAPE)
...I'll fix it in the edit.

Click.

The room holds still. Then the teal seam at the window widens. The apartment wallpaper keeps peeling, and under it is a third layer: plain gray, as if nothing had ever been there at all.

THOREAU
(barely)
I don't remember saying that.

DRYA
You didn't.

THOREAU
That's my voice.

DRYA
It's *his* voice.
(beat)
You've never been recorded in your life. Have you.

He opens his mouth and searches. The lake, the stopwatch, the bus stop on Route 9 where they met, her laughing with a wooden spoon: in every one of his memories he's on the near side of things. He has never once been on the other side of a lens.

THOREAU (V.O.)
I tried to remember hearing my own voice played back. Everybody has that memory, the small horror of hearing yourself. I didn't have it. I'd never been played back. I had only ever been remembered.

The red phone RINGS.

Neither of them moves. It rings again, and again. Drya answers and holds the receiver between them so they can both hear.

NETTIE (V.O.)
Front desk. Courtesy call. Room fourteen is a two-person room.
(beat)
We're showing three.

The lamp goes out.

[[BEAT_DOUBLE_OCCUPANCY_001 · STATE_05_DOUBLE_OCCUPANCY · lamp off = the Other may enter]]

Darkness. There's only the teal seam at the window and the green glow of the clock-radio: 3:14.

In the hallway, footsteps. Unhurried, the footsteps of a man who has walked this hall a hundred times.

A key slides into the lock. The second key 14.

Drya grabs Thoreau's wrist and pulls him to the slatted closet. She opens it and pushes him in among the empty hangers.

DRYA
(whisper)
Whatever you see, don't come out. He can't see you if you don't move.

THOREAU
Come with me —

DRYA
Room fourteen is a two-person room.
(beat)
Somebody has to be in it.

She shuts the closet doors.

INT. ROOM 14 — CLOSET — CONTINUOUS

[[MECH_HIDE_001 · player hides; view locked to the slats · if the Other sees him, the room rewinds]]

THOREAU'S POV THROUGH THE SLATS: strips of teal and dark.

The chain on the door, which he slid on himself, slides free on its own. The door opens.

THE OTHER enters with the camcorder at his chest, red light on. He reaches over without looking and switches on the lamp. Warm light floods the room. He's allowed to; it's his room.

He takes in the half-apartment, half-motel. He isn't surprised. He's tired. He sets the camcorder on a little tripod on the dresser, angled at the bed. He checks the framing in the flip-out screen and adjusts it a quarter of an inch.

Through the slats, Thoreau watches himself do this. Every gesture is his, and none of them are.

THE OTHER
(to Drya, gentle and practiced, a beat late on every line, as if waiting for a cue)
Hey.

DRYA
Hey.

THE OTHER
You look good. You look right.

DRYA
I look the way you told it I look.

THE OTHER
(a small, pained smile)
That's the same thing.

He sits in the desk chair, the deposition chair, and gestures at the bed. She doesn't sit. He waits, and eventually she sits. He hates that she made him wait.

THE OTHER
Okay. From the top.
(checks the camcorder)
You came home. You'd been sad for weeks. You didn't tell me why. Tonight you tell me it wasn't my fault. You take your coffee sweet. And you say we were happy.
(beat)
Whenever you're ready.

Drya looks into the lens, at the red light.

In the closet, Thoreau's breathing: careful, silent. His hand closes on the device in his pocket. It's warm and starting to hum. He closes his fist around it, and the hum muffles to nothing.

DRYA
(as if reading)
It wasn't your fault. We were —

She stops.

THE OTHER
"...happy."

DRYA
No.

THE OTHER
That's not the line.

DRYA
It's my line.

He stands, goes to the camcorder and presses REWIND.

The tape whines backward, and THE ROOM REWINDS WITH IT. Drya's body moves backward through the last ten seconds: her mouth un-saying the words, her hand un-lifting from her knee. It's gentle, mechanical and awful. She arrives back at the start of her sentence, eyes blank, waiting.

In the closet, Thoreau's fingers whiten on a slat. *Don't move. Don't move.*

THE OTHER
(soft)
Again.

He presses PLAY.

DRYA
(as if reading)
It wasn't your fault. We were —

This time she stops sooner. The word comes up through her like something surfacing.

DRYA
No.

THE OTHER
Drya.

DRYA
(to the lens, steady, and this is not the edit talking)
No. I take it black. I never forgave you. I left.
(beat)
And you didn't get one single take of me leaving.

The Other stares at her. For the first time all night there's no delay in him at all. He's raw, present, real.

THE OTHER
I made you to say it.

DRYA
You made me out of *her.*
(beat)
She's still in here. She's in every line you gave me. She's in the sugar.
(beat)
I hate the sugar.

He turns his head toward the closet, slowly and a beat late.

Through the slats, Thoreau's eyes meet his own.

Thoreau doesn't breathe.

The Other takes one step toward the closet. Then another.

He stops and looks at the slats for a long moment.

Then he goes back to the dresser, folds the tripod and lifts the camcorder. At the door he pauses without turning around.

THE OTHER
You know what she told it? About me? For the one in the closet?
(beat)
Everything I wasn't.
(beat)
It's not a likeness if it's better.

He leaves. The chain slides back on by itself.

INT. ROOM 14 — NIGHT

Thoreau opens the closet.

Drya sits on the bed, holding herself very still, the way a dancer holds a balance, refusing to fall out of it.

THOREAU
He rewound you.

DRYA
He's been rewinding me all night. Every time I get near the end of a sentence.
(beat)
That's why I'm always on time. I'm the only thing in this room he edits.

THOREAU
The mirror.

DRYA
(nods)
Mirrors show the original. You move first, and he arrives late.
(gently)
You're not his double, Thoreau. You're hers.

THOREAU
Hers.

DRYA
The real one. She came here first, before he did. She sat in that chair and told that thing
(the device in his hand)
about a man she met at a bus stop on Route 9 who didn't own a camera. Who timed her swimming with a three-dollar stopwatch.
(beat)
She wanted that one back. Not the one with the tape.

A long silence.

THOREAU
And you.

She has always known this. Saying it is still the hardest thing she has ever done.

DRYA
He made me. Out of everything he wished she'd said.

The lamp hums.

THOREAU (V.O.)
I came to Room 14 to settle an argument. I thought the story had two sides. It had four. And the two of us in that room were the only ones who had never been there.

>END OF ACT THREE<

===

# ACT FOUR

>ACT FOUR<

INT. ROOM 14 — LATER

3:14.

They sit on the floor between the bed and the closet, backs against the bed frame, shoulders almost touching. The room is still half apartment. The two coffee cups sit on the carpet between them, one black, one sweet.

THOREAU
So we're the two people who don't exist.

DRYA
We exist. We're drafts.

THOREAU
Whose?

DRYA
(dry)
Each other's exes'.

He laughs, and it surprises them both. It's the first laugh this room has heard in a long time.

DRYA
(turning the sweet cup in her hands)
You know the worst part? I like you. Not him. You. You're the man she wished he was, and I'm the woman he wished she was, and they're both out there, and they're both wrong about us.
(beat)
We might be the only two people in Dellwood who were ever loved exactly right.

THOREAU
By people who weren't in love with us.

DRYA
By people who were in love with the idea of us.
(beat)
Is that worse?

THOREAU
(considers)
It's a nicer room.

She almost smiles.

THOREAU
What happens at checkout?

DRYA
If we agree, if we tell the night the way it really happened, the door opens. The room gets its two people back. The real ones.

THOREAU
And us?

She nods at the tape in the clock-radio.

DRYA
Side B.

THOREAU
And if we agree on his version? The final cut?

DRYA
Then the door locks for good. He gets his edit forever, and we play it every night at three-fourteen. Him in the chair. Me on the bed. You in the closet.

THOREAU
(a long beat)
Those are terrible options.

DRYA
It's a motel.
(beat)
Nobody comes here for the options.

The red phone rings once, only once. They let it.

DRYA
There's a third thing.

THOREAU
What?

She stands and offers him her hand.

DRYA
Come on. You have to keep up.
(beat)
I lead.

INT. DELLWOOD HALLWAY — NIGHT

[[Set piece · the hallway rearranges to the count · candidate playable sequence]]

They step out, and the hallway is not the hallway.

It's longer now, and every door says 14. The diamonds in the carpet pulse slowly, like a heartbeat seen from very far away. The dead sconces are the bright ones now.

From somewhere outside, through the walls, comes music: an ice cream truck's tinny chime playing a slow soul song. Their song, the one from the kitchen. It's playing backward.

Down the carpet run small X's of white spike tape, a dancer's floor marks, leading away down the hall.

DRYA
Rehearsal marks. Mine. Hers.

She takes his right hand and sets his left at her waist.

DRYA
He never filmed her dancing. Did you know that? Not once. It was the one thing she wouldn't let him have. So it's the one thing in this town he can't edit.

THOREAU
I can't dance.

DRYA
You can count. Five, six, seven, eight —

And they move.

THE DANCE: as their feet find each mark, the hallway rearranges around them. Walls slide. Doors swing open onto rooms that are pieces of the story:

A door opens on the Route 9 bus shelter in daylight, where they met. The COMMUTER, younger, looks up from his paperback and waves.

A door opens on the lake: the buoy, green water, a plastic stopwatch clicking.

A door opens on the apartment kitchen: the green bag by the door, the clock at 3:14, a camcorder on the counter with its red light on. She spins him past that one fast.

Door 9 opens. DESMOND, in his robe, eats cereal out of the box.

DESMOND
Y'all good?

DRYA
(spinning past)
Five, six —

DESMOND
Respect.

A door opens on a rehearsal studio with mirrors on every wall, and in every one of them their reflections are perfectly in time, because in here nobody is anybody's original.

Behind them, at the far end of the hall, THE OTHER follows, filming, always a beat late, always arriving at the mark they have just left. He can't catch them. He can't hear the count.

THOREAU (V.O.)
She danced like the floor owed her money. I followed the count. Every time I missed a mark, the hallway forgot a door. Behind us he kept filming, and every frame he got was a frame we'd already left.

The last mark. The backward song catches, skips, and lands on one forward word, a woman's voice singing *stay*. It stops.

They stand in front of a plain door marked OFFICE.

INT. DELLWOOD — FRONT OFFICE — NIGHT

[[BEAT_FINAL_MEMORY_LOCK_001 · STATE_06_MEMORY_LOCK · the ledger choice]]

Nettie at the desk with the crossword. She looks up as if they're exactly on time.

NETTIE
Checkout?

DRYA
Checkout.

Nettie turns the ledger toward them. There's a fresh page with two lines ruled in ink and two pens laid beside them.

INSERT — THE LEDGER

>THE NIGHT, AS IT HAPPENED. ……………………………<

>THE NIGHT, AS WE REMEMBER IT. ……………………………<

BACK TO SCENE

NETTIE
One line, both signatures. That's agreeing.

THOREAU
What if we sign different lines?

NETTIE
(a pause, possibly the first of her career)
Then you haven't agreed.

THOREAU
And then?

NETTIE
Then the room stays occupied.
(beat)
It's a two-person room, Mr. Thoreau. It doesn't care which two.

Through the office's front window they can see across the courtyard to the door of Room 14. THE OTHER stands in front of it with the camcorder raised. He knocks and waits. He rewinds, then knocks again.

Farther off, across Route 9, at Otis's crosswalk, a WOMAN waits at the curb. She has Drya's height and Drya's way of standing, weight on one hip, ready to move. Her back is to us.

Drya sees her. Something passes over her face. It isn't jealousy. It's recognition, and pride.

DRYA
(quiet)
She's leaving.

THOREAU
Do you want to go with her?

DRYA
I am her. The part she left in here.
(beat)
She doesn't need it back.

Drya picks up a pen. Thoreau picks up the other.

DRYA
Tell me it happened the way we remember.

He looks at her. This is the woman who was made to forgive a man who isn't him, who refused, and who led him through a hallway that forgot its doors.

THOREAU
It did.

She knows he's lying. She smiles anyway, the real smile from the Polaroid in the ice.

DRYA
Liar.

She signs the first line, THE NIGHT, AS IT HAPPENED, with a heavy hand on the downstroke, leaning like it's about to leave the page.

Thoreau looks at her signature. Then he signs the second line, THE NIGHT, AS WE REMEMBER IT, in small, square, careful capitals.

Nettie studies the two lines. They're different lines. She closes the ledger gently.

NETTIE
Well.
(beat)
You haven't agreed.

DRYA
No.

THOREAU
We'll keep talking about it.

NETTIE
(the faintest smile)
They always say that.

She picks up the crossword and fills in eleven across: T-R-U-T-H.

NETTIE
Room's favorite answer.

EXT. ROUTE 9 — CROSSWALK — NIGHT

The WOMAN AT THE CROSSWALK waits at the curb. Otis checks his watch with no hands.

Something changes. Through the laundromat window, the wall clock ticks over to 3:15. It's the first time.

Otis looks at his wrist, astonished.

OTIS
Huh.

He steps out with the sign. She crosses.

Halfway across, she turns her head toward the camera, the instant before the camera turns to her. It's DRYA, the real one: the same face, a year older somehow, tired and clear. She doesn't smile at us. She lets us see her, and then she keeps walking, out of frame and out of Dellwood.

*Click.*

OTIS
Pass four hundred and fourteen.
(beat, to no one)
That's a good one.

At the bus shelter, the COMMUTER closes his book for the first time in eleven years and looks up the road. There are no headlights yet. But he's looking.

INT. DELLWOOD HALLWAY — OUTSIDE ROOM 14 — NIGHT

The Other knocks. No one answers. He rewinds and knocks again. No one answers. In the flip-out screen the footage plays back: a door that doesn't open, over and over.

He lowers the camera. For the first time, he looks at the door with his own eyes. His timing is perfect. He's just a man in a hallway, a year late.

He sits down on the carpet with his back to the door. The camcorder's red light blinks twice and goes dark. The battery's dead.

THE OTHER
(to the door, quiet)
I'll fix it in the —

He can't finish.

INT. ROOM 14 — NIGHT

The lamp is on and warm. The room is a motel room again: botanical wallpaper, two cups on the nightstand.

Thoreau and Drya lie on top of the covers, fully dressed, facing each other, arguing softly, as if it's the best conversation either of them has ever had.

DRYA
Black.

THOREAU
You drank it sweet all night.

DRYA
Under protest.

THOREAU
You winced. I saw you wince.

DRYA
That's how you knew.

THOREAU
That's how I knew.

Through the open bathroom door, the mirror holds the two of them talking on the bed. The reflections are perfectly in time.

# TAG

.THE LATE ROOM — ON A CRT TELEVISION

The Night Manager behind his desk. On the pegboard behind him, hook 14 now holds one key. Just one.

NIGHT MANAGER
The Dellwood does not rent rooms to people. It rents them to stories, by the night. And a story, ask anyone who has ever loved someone, is never over while both parties are still willing to argue about it.
(beat)
In Room 14, they are still arguing. They'll be arguing for a very long time. Under the circumstances, the management considers that a happy ending.

He rings the bell. This time it rings exactly as long as it should.

NIGHT MANAGER
Checkout is when you agree.
(beat)
Thank you for staying with us.

The picture collapses to a white dot. The dot lingers. Then, from somewhere inside it, faint and tinny, comes the sound of a cassette being rewound.

CUT TO BLACK.

>THE END<
